//
// THE SCRAMBLE — the run's ending, and the only one there is.
//
// [`plans/28`](../plans/28-the-scramble/README.md) S1.  `docs/DESIGN.md`
// § 2 names the game after this: *scramble-and-salvage*, where a base is
// one round of a longer run and the player launches the central building
// as a rocket carrying whatever they managed to grab.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ The rule this file exists to keep ────────────────────────
//
// > **The base never ends itself.  It is always the player's decision
// > to stop** (`@X293`, owner 2026-08-28).
//
// ⚠ Not merely *the player is not forced out by damage* — **there is no
// state at all in which the game takes the decision away.**  The wallet
// at zero is poverty and the base plays on; the wave list running out is
// `DESIGN.md`'s *free scramble PHASE* and not a finish; an expired
// permit costs the CARGO and never the launch; and a destroyed vehicle
// respawns at the core and arms this countdown — **which exiting
// cancels**, which is the one place the game comes closest to deciding
// and hands it straight back.
//
// ⚠⚠ **So nothing here may hurry the player**: no auto-launch, no hard
// timer, no *"nothing left to do"* prompt.  A player who stays to watch
// something is using the game as intended.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ WHERE "inside the core" IS, and what S0 measured ─────────
//
// `DESIGN.md` § 4 describes a 7-hex prism with six faces and an
// *opening* the vehicle drives **through** into an interior.  ⚠ **None
// of that exists**: `plans/28` § S0 measured that the core is ONE marker
// on ONE hex of ordinary ground, and a drive straight across it does not
// notice — **a marker is not terrain.**
//
// ⚠⚠ **So the position is the core's OWN HEX, and it earns its place by
// being distinguishable from the one every other core interaction
// uses.**  `beacon_buy` and `wave_drop` work *within `CARGO_REACH_HEXES`*
// — beside the core.  The scramble is tighter because the fiction is
// *drive in*:
//
// > **Beside the core you TRADE; on the core you LEAVE.**
//
// ⚠ And `vehicle.loft::vehicle_respawn` already puts the player exactly
// there, so `DESIGN.md` § 4's *"vehicle death starts the launch
// countdown automatically — a free 'ready to leave?' prompt"* falls out
// with no code at all.  That is the strongest evidence the hex is right.

use lattice;
use vehicle;
use helper;
use wallet;
use fixstep;

// How long the player must stay inside before the rocket fires.
//
// `examples/numbers.json` § core.launch_countdown_duration — 6.0 s, with
// the note *"bottom pulse beats faster as it elapses.  Exit cancels."*
//
// ⚠⚠ **In integer base units, and that is `plans/26`'s whole family**
// (`@D004`): a duration spelled in seconds through an accumulator comes
// back a tick short, so the constant is converted ONCE here and the
// `Timer` counts units.  ⚠ 6.0 s is **exactly nine ticks** at
// `TICK_STEP_UNITS` with no remainder, which is what makes the reading
// the same at every tick length the sweep uses.
pub const LAUNCH_COUNTDOWN_SECONDS: float = 6.0;

// The launch, as a state.
//
// ⚠ A struct rather than two loose fields on `WaveState`, for the reason
// `Jammer` is one: the polarity is a trap, so the rule lives in one
// place with the doors that state it the right way round.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`gone` stores FALSE for a base still being played**, so
// [loft#914]'s silent default on a partial `Scramble { }` literal is
// today's game — the same discipline `jammer.off` and
// `tower.top_removed` follow.
pub struct Scramble {
    count: Timer,
    gone:  boolean,
}

// Factory: a base nobody has left yet.
pub fn scramble_new() -> Scramble {
    Scramble { count: timer_new(), gone: false }
}

// Has the player launched?  ⚠ **Every reader asks this and never the
// field**, so the one place the polarity is written is here.
pub fn scramble_gone(sc: const Scramble) -> boolean {
    sc.gone
}

// Is the countdown running — the player is inside and has not left?
pub fn scramble_counting(sc: const Scramble) -> boolean {
    timer_running(sc.count)
}

// How much of the countdown is left, in base units.  `0` when it is not
// running, which is meaningless rather than wrong — ask
// `scramble_counting` first, exactly as with `flow_distance`.
pub fn scramble_left(sc: const Scramble) -> integer {
    timer_left(sc.count)
}

// Is the player standing ON the core — the position that launches?
//
// ⚠ **Parked, not merely at the coordinates.**  A vehicle that has never
// been placed sits at (0, 0) and is absent (`vehicle.loft` § parked), so
// a core at the origin would otherwise read as occupied by a player who
// is not in the world.
pub fn vehicle_in_core(v: const Vehicle, core: const Hex) -> boolean {
    if !vehicle_present(v) { return false; }
    v.q == core.q && v.r == core.r
}

// One tick of the launch.  Answers whether the rocket fired THIS tick.
//
// ⚠⚠ **Entering arms it, leaving CANCELS it, and cancelling resets** —
// `DESIGN.md` § 4: *"Exiting the opening at any time before liftoff
// cancels the sequence — pulse fades, countdown resets."*  So a player
// who steps out at five seconds and back in starts from six again;
// there is no banked progress, and that is the design's word rather than
// a simplification.
//
// ⚠ **It does not pause the wave**, and must not: § 4 calls the
// countdown *a hazard window* — enemies that reach the core keep
// nibbling while the player sits inside.  Nothing here touches the
// schedule, and `plans/28` S5 asserts it.
//
// ⚠ Once gone, it stays gone: a second call answers false rather than
// firing again, which is what makes the manifest a conservation
// (`plans/28` S3).
// Example: @DRY-138
pub fn scramble_step(sc: Scramble, inside: boolean, elapsed: integer) -> boolean {
    if sc.gone { return false; }

    if !inside {
        // ⚠ Cancel unconditionally rather than only when running: a
        // `timer_cancel` on a stopped timer is a no-op, and a guard here
        // would be a second place the running-ness is decided.
        timer_cancel(sc.count);
        return false;
    }

    if !timer_running(sc.count) {
        timer_arm(sc.count, clock_units_from_seconds(LAUNCH_COUNTDOWN_SECONDS));
        // ⚠ Armed THIS tick and not yet spent — a countdown that fired
        // on the tick it started would make the duration a lie at any
        // tick length where one tick covers it.
        return false;
    }

    if timer_spend(sc.count, elapsed) {
        sc.gone = true;
        return true;
    }
    false
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ The manifest — what left with them (S3) ─────────────────
//
// `DESIGN.md` § 14 § Scramble exit + cargo manifest: *at launch, the
// cargo manifest is **whatever made it onboard*** — the wallet, the tops
// deposited at the core, the helpers who had boarded, and the loot
// helpers had delivered.  ⚠ *Force-launch leaves stragglers behind — by
// design.  The cost of haste.*
//
// ⚠⚠ **Two of the four have nothing to read yet, and this file says so
// rather than pretending**: there is no *deposit a top at the core*
// anywhere in the tree (a top's destinations are a tower marker and a
// carrier, `carry.loft` § A top has TWO destinations), and delivered
// loot goes straight into the wallet the moment it is cleared
// (`wave_tick`'s salvage), so it is already counted in the points.
// **What is left is exactly the two the game can answer**, and they are
// the two the player feels.

// How far from the core's centre a crew member counts as ABOARD.
//
// ⚠ `examples/numbers.json` § core.footprint_layout is
// `hex_disc_radius_1` — the core's own 7-hex footprint — so this is the
// building's extent rather than a number this file invented.
//
// ⚠⚠ **It is deliberately WIDER than the player's own rule.**  The
// player must be on the CENTRE hex, because `DESIGN.md` § 4 has them
// drive *through the opening into the interior*; a crew member merely
// has to have reached the rocket.  ⚠ The generosity is the honest proxy
// for a thing that is not built: § 4 has helpers *board on their own
// when their work list is empty*, and until they do, standing at the
// core is what boarding looks like.
pub const ABOARD_REACH_HEXES: integer = 1;

// What left with the player.
//
// ⚠ **`left` is carried rather than derived at the call site**, because
// the two halves are one claim: `plans/28` § Invariant gate makes the
// manifest a CONSERVATION — aboard plus left behind is the roster — and
// a caller that computed one from the other could not fail the check.
pub struct Manifest {
    points: float,
    crew:   integer,
    left:   integer,
}

// Is this crew member close enough to the core to have boarded?
pub fn crew_aboard(h: const Helper, core: const Hex) -> boolean {
    if !h.alive { return false; }
    lat_distance(core, Hex { q: h.q, r: h.r }) <= ABOARD_REACH_HEXES
}

// The manifest, as the rocket leaves.
//
// ⚠ It reads a state rather than mutating one, so it answers the same
// thing before and after the launch and a scenario may ask it twice.
// ⚠⚠ **The points are the wallet UNSPENT** — `numbers.json`
// § economy.carryover_points_ratio is 1.0, *"all unspent points carry"*
// — so there is no conversion here and there must not be one: a ratio
// applied twice is the class of defect `plans/26` spent four plans on.
//
// ⚠ A crew member who is WRECKED is neither aboard nor left behind:
// they are already out of the roster's living count, and `plans/14` H3
// keeps their slot so *retrieval is the only way back*.  The
// conservation is therefore over the LIVING, and § S3's gate says so.
// ⚠⚠ **It takes the PIECES and not the `WaveState`, and that is
// structural rather than taste**: `spawn.loft` uses this file, so this
// file cannot see `WaveState` — a `use` imports one way only.  The same
// constraint shaped `moat.loft::moat_depth`, and the same benefit
// follows: the manifest can be asked about a roster and a wallet that
// are not in a run at all, which is what makes it testable.
// Example: @DRY-139
pub fn manifest_of(w: const Wallet, crew: const vector<Helper>,
                   core: const Hex) -> Manifest {
    mo_on  = 0;
    mo_off = 0;
    for mo_h in crew {
        if mo_h.alive {
            if crew_aboard(mo_h, core) { mo_on += 1; } else { mo_off += 1; }
        }
    }
    Manifest {
        points: wallet_left(w),
        crew:   mo_on,
        left:   mo_off,
    }
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ The chain — a manifest OPENS the next base (`plans/31` N4) ──
//
// `DESIGN.md` § 14 § Base sequence: *a run is a **sequence of bases**,
// chained by what the player carries out.*  `manifest_of` above has
// answered *what left with them* since `plans/28` S3, and until this
// function nothing read it: outside this file and its own test,
// `manifest` appeared in `src/` exactly once, in a comment.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THIS IS WHERE THE REFUSALS LIVE, and they are the design's own
// rather than a scope cut.**  Enforces @FR-R-Opening — *nothing
// survives a sortie boundary that is not in the manifest* — and the
// manifest has three columns of which exactly one crosses today:
//
//   `points` — crosses, 1:1.  `numbers.json`
//              § economy.carryover_points_ratio is 1.0.
//   `crew`   — does NOT.  `DESIGN.md` § 9 § Roster: **2 starting, and
//              the two are FIXED** (`@X258`); between missions the
//              roster is managed at a STATION that does not exist
//              (`PROGRESSION.md` § P2i).  ⚠⚠ And `@X290`: **a crew
//              member has no identity outside one run's roster**, so a
//              COUNT that crossed would carry the number while losing
//              the people — the half the design cares about.
//   `left`   — does NOT.  § 9 § Stranded helpers is explicit: *"For
//              validation: stranded is a data state only; the
//              rescue-quest UI is deferred."*  It is a RECORD of what
//              happened, never an input.
//
// ⚠ Each refusal is blocked on a thing with a NAME — a station, an
// identity, a deposit — which is what `@X324` asks of a piece left out:
// *a piece dropped for convenience is a regression even when everything
// still works.*
//
// ⚠⚠ **And the CONVERSION happens exactly once.**  Enforces
// @FR-R-Carry-Once.  `manifest_of` refuses to convert and says so in
// its own header; `wallet_carrying` is the only place a ratio could
// ever be applied.  So a carry applied twice is not a bug to be avoided
// by review — it is a state the pair cannot reach.
pub fn manifest_opens(m: const Manifest) -> Wallet {
    wallet_carrying(m.points)
}
